match	cd /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/git-templates\ngit status --short\ngit checkout -q -b fix/propagate-double-count 2>/dev/null || git checkout -q fix/propagate-double-count\ngit add -A && git commit -q -F - <<'MSG'\nfix: stop double-counting in the fleet sweep\n\nThe in-process change added a step to remove hand-written sub-hooks,\nbut it matched OUR files too — which step 1 already retires. Every file\nwas listed twice, so the dry run reported 192 removals per hook name\nwhere there were 96.\n\nHarmless to `rm -f`, misleading to read, and exactly the kind of\nduplicated condition that later grows two different meanings. The\nforeign step now skips anything is_ours() claims, and reports its own\ncount so the two are never conflated again.\n\nCaught by reading the dry run rather than trusting it — 192 is not a\nnumber 96 repos can produce for a file each holds once.\nMSG\ngit push -q -u origin fix/propagate-double-count 2>&1|tail -1\ngh pr create --title "fix: stop double-counting in the fleet sweep" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'\nThe in-process change added a step to remove hand-written sub-hooks, but it matched **our** files too — which step 1 already retires. Every file was listed twice: the dry run reported `192` removals per hook name across 96 repos that hold one each.\n\nHarmless to `rm -f`, misleading to read, and the kind of duplicated condition that later grows two different meanings. The foreign step now skips anything `is_ours()` claims and reports its own count.\n\nCaught by reading the dry-run output rather than trusting it — 192 is not a number 96 repos can produce for a file each holds once. The sweep was then applied with the corrected script: 1520 stale shims, 2 foreign, 384 installed, 0 leftovers.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\nEOF\n)" 2>&1 | tail -1
match	cd /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/git-templates\nT=$(mktemp -d); cd $T; git init -q .; git config user.email t@t; git config user.name t\ncp /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/git-templates/.git/hooks/* .git/hooks/\nprintf '[package]\\nname="t"\\nversion="0.1.0"\\nedition="2021"\\n' > Cargo.toml\nmkdir -p src && printf 'fn main(){let _x=1;}\\n' > src/main.rs\ngit add -A\necho "=== unformatted Rust must be refused by the INSTALLED binary ==="\n./.git/hooks/pre-commit 2>&1 | sed 's/\\x1b\\[[0-9;]*m//g' | grep -E "Rust|Clippy|✗" | head -3\ncd /; rm -rf $T\ncd /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/git-templates\ngit checkout -q -b fix/install-guard-fails-closed\ngit add Makefile && git commit -q -F - <<'MSG'\nfix: make the install guard fail closed\n\n`make install` has now destroyed tracked source TWICE. The first guard\ncompared paths against $(MAKEFILE_DIR), which a git worktree defeats.\nThe second asked `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` and deleted whenever\nthat command was unhappy — so one hiccup in an unrelated git invocation\nwas enough to wipe templates/hooks/, which is exactly what happened\nwhile merging #38.\n\nBoth shared the same flaw: they FAILED OPEN. Anything the check could\nnot confirm became "safe to delete".\n\nNow every branch that is not positive proof of safety refuses, and the\nFIRST question asked is the one that actually matters: does git TRACK\nanything here? An install step must never delete a tracked file, whatever\nthe path resolution says. `rm -v ... || true` is also gone — a failing\ndelete should be visible, not swallowed.\n\nVerified: `make install` now prints "holds TRACKED files — leaving it\nalone" and the working tree stays clean.\nMSG\ngit push -q -u origin fix/install-guard-fails-closed 2>&1 | grep -E "✗" | head -2\ngh pr create --title "fix: make the install guard fail closed" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'\n`make install` has now destroyed tracked source **twice**.\n\n- The first guard compared paths against `$(MAKEFILE_DIR)` — a git **worktree** defeats it, since the two resolve to different checkouts of the same repo.\n- The second asked `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` and deleted whenever that command was unhappy. One hiccup in an unrelated git invocation was enough to wipe `templates/hooks/` — which is what happened while merging #38.\n\nBoth shared one flaw: **they failed open.** Anything the check could not confirm became "safe to delete".\n\nNow every branch that isn't positive proof of safety refuses, and the first question asked is the one that actually matters:\n\n```\ndoes git TRACK anything here?  → refuse\nis it inside any checkout?     → refuse\ncan't resolve the path?        → refuse\ngit not on PATH?               → refuse\n```\n\nAn install step must never delete a tracked file, whatever the path resolution says. `rm -v ... || true` is gone too — a failing delete should be visible, not swallowed.\n\nVerified: `make install` prints `holds TRACKED files — leaving it alone`, the working tree stays clean, and the four templates survive.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\nEOF\n)" 2>&1 | tail -1
